WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:06.460
Jay Fingers, Screenwriter/Youtuber: I think
it's undeniable every every film every story

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:08.230
has that moment

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:14.740
Film Courage: What is your writing process
as I mentioned I have ideas rattling in my

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:21.680
head when I decide that I want to work on
something the first thing that I do is I get

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:28.380
a composition notebook usually like one of
those black and white speckled notebooks and

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:33.760
that's a holdover from junior high my writing
teacher that's what she made us you know we

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.350
had to get the black and white speckle notebook
that's where all of our assignments you know

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:46.030
we did them in that notebook so that's just
stuck with me to this day I get a notebook

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:53.100
write the working title on the cover and on
the first page first page is reserved for

00:00:53.100 --> 00:01:00.539
the title and character names I usually start
with the protagonist right once I've got that

00:01:00.539 --> 00:01:07.460
notebook every idea every thought that I have
about this story goes into that notebook there's

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:12.040
no real rhyme or reason I don't you know aside
from the first page I don't have say like

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:18.160
a section for dialogue or anything like that
you know I just everything that I've been

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:24.590
thinking goes into that notebook if I go out
to have lunch or whatever and something hits

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:32.750
me that I think would be perfect I come back
and I put in that notebook once I have kind

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:40.970
of like all the notes and everything I craft
my outline my outlines are not too detailed

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:47.640
you know they're pretty spare and this is
because once I have the outline I start writing

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:54.610
the story really really like develops it really
begins to tell itself you know I've got my

00:01:54.610 --> 00:02:01.159
notes I've got the outline I know what's supposed
to happen but it blossoms during the writing

00:02:01.159 --> 00:02:07.210
process so I like to you know keep things
loose the outline is like you know very spare

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:14.760
and I mean that's pretty much it you know
I write I can't write out of order i'm not

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:19.890
one of those writers I know a lot of people
that say oh if a scene is hard for you you

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:26.580
know just write tktk and come back to it I
can't do that I have to work it out in that

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:31.860
moment and if i'm not able to work it out
in that moment then that's where I stop for

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:39.390
the day and when I come back to it the next
day or whatever let's give it another go and

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:44.050
and yeah that's that's pretty much my process
and I mean that's that's for my my books as

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:52.720
well you know all of my books same thing get
the notebook write the characters throw in

00:02:52.720 --> 00:03:02.380
whatever notes outline it and then write and
are you reading the new work from the day

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:06.910
before like are you going through the entire
thing of what you've written or you're just

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:11.190
starting from the scene right about where
you're going to start up again yeah I just

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:15.330
start from the scene the thing is I know the
story by the time I start writing the actual

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:21.590
script I know the story so well I don't have
to review the previous day's pages or you

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:26.980
know start from scratch I don't have to do
any of that I just jump in at the at the scene

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:32.870
and I keep going and then once the script
is finished then you know of course I go back

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:37.540
and I read from the beginning and see and
usually things things work out I mean there

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:44.410
may be some things that I'd have to shift
or you know whatever maybe there's a payoff

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:48.760
that you know didn't have a setup and so I
may need to include that or whatever but for

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:55.340
the most part you know because I know the
story so well I can just jump right in and

00:03:55.340 --> 00:04:01.701
then when you finished the screenplay do you
give it some time before you go in and look

00:04:01.701 --> 00:04:06.090
at okay definitely definitely you know it's
the old put it in the drawer don't think about

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:13.370
it I don't even give it to people to read
at that point you know I just let it sit and

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:18.190
get to work on the next idea and then I pull
it out and you know once I pull it out you

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:22.690
know after some time that's when i'll give
it to readers you know because it's kind of

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:29.690
fresh for everyone if that makes sense so
get the feedback from my readers and you know

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:34.530
I've given it time giving it some distance
and it's just like okay I also find out more

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:41.370
receptive to to getting like feedback and
notes because that time has passed and i'm

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:45.500
a little bit removed from it you know because
when you're you know I've just finished it

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:51.210
you know it's my baby if I get notes back
